A||||6||MooCow||Which Fiero to choose?????||||5||
Z||000000||MooCow||01-31-2000||07:56 PM||||As you all may know in April I will have about $5,000 for my first Fiero. I promise I will post pics in case I need help on fixing it like Monkeyman says. Now I want your opinons on which Fiero to choose.  Should I get an 86 SE v6?<BR>get an 86.5 GT v6?<BR>get a 87GT v6?<BR>get a 88GT v6?<BR>all fully loaded with an auto-matic around 75,000 I hope. Also which would be easiest on to find parts for??<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||01-31-2000||08:31 PM||||Ride and handling, go with the 88, parts availability go with the 86/87. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||01-31-2000||08:49 PM||||If you can find a nice '88 for that price, definately go for it!  However, if you find a excellent 86.5-87 GT or SE, with low miles... you can probably get more for your dollar.||||reg||6
Z||000003||stimpy||01-31-2000||09:29 PM||||If you do wind up spending the whole nut on initial purchase price, you really shouldn't have to do any repairs one would hope. As far as what to buy, sure, I would love to have an 88 v6,  but I enjoy my 86 gt immensely. My origial purchase price was $2500 and $3000 bought me a fresh healthy "built" 2.8. The parts are mostly all interchagable from 85 to 87 which makes junkyard huntngg for bits and pieces a little less challenging.  I would recommend that you stay away from dealers and spend your 5 large w/ a knowledgeable car builder with a good reputation. Good luck!  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||02-01-2000||01:45 AM||||You're getting good advice here (above anyhow... I tend to ramble  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ). Part of the decision is personal preference.  You should be able to find any of them for $5,000 or less in good shape.<P>If you like the fastback, go with one of the GTs. The 88s will run a little higher price and some parts are a little harder to find  but they do have better suspension and brakes. <P>If you like the notchback the 85 GT or 86 SE would be good. Also, an 88 Formula will give you the same drive train and suspension as an 88 GT but in a notchback body. And they tend to run a little cheaper than the 88 GT. <P>Figure out what is really important to you and make sure the car you get has it or it is easy to change. Replacing interior parts, seats, radio or even adding power windows is probably something a courageous novice could tackle. Changing engines, transmissions or adding T-Tops on the other hand...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Here are some links to some price guides to give you an idea what you can expect for your money. NOTE: prices will vary greatly... these are just to give you some idea...  <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.net/prices.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.net/prices.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~paulv/Price%20Guide.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~paulv/Price%20Guide.htm</A>  <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 02-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||JIM GOOSE||02-01-2000||07:53 AM||||Get the last & best with most options and least mileage that balances with the price. It doesnt matter what kind of car ,usually applies anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||02-01-2000||08:51 AM||||With a $5K budget, I'd be looking at the following:<P>1) A perfect 86-87 GT<BR>2) A perfect 88 Formula<BR>3) A very nice 88 GT (I don't think you'll find perfection for $5K)<BR>4) A perfect 85 GT or 86-87 SE and $1K left over<BR>5) A very nice 85-87 Coupe w/auto and air for $1500 - then have a 3800 V6 installed<BR>||||reg||1
